Apple ISP capture delivery stress
=================================

Build with make -C tools/testing/selftests/media. This is a manual hardware
runner; run_tests does not start the camera automatically. No image files are
written. Run only on the agreed J616s test kernel while audio is idle.

Example for configurations whose expected rate has been established as 30 fps:

  ./apple-isp-stress /dev/video0 600 10 30 | tee capture-results.jsonl

It enumerates every offered single-planar format and discrete size. Each gets
a ten-minute run, then ten five-second start/stop cycles, switching formats and
sizes between streams. A substituted format/rate, poll timeout, queue error,
V4L2 buffer error, sequence discontinuity or delivery gap exceeding 1.5 nominal
frame periods fails. Delivered FPS must reach 99.8% of the supplied rate, allowing
29.97/30 rounding. The rate argument is an explicit test expectation, not a
rate inferred from the sensor clock or set by the tool as an alleged sensor fact.
For modes with a different nominal rate, filter the run explicitly, for example
`./apple-isp-stress /dev/video0 600 10 25 P010 1920 1080`. A filtered run tests
that mode's restart cycles; the unfiltered run also exercises format switches. This runner stops
on the first failure to preserve evidence and avoid retrying ambiguous firmware.

The driver's sequence increments for received reports, so consecutive sequence
values alone cannot establish zero sensor/firmware drops. The timing checks
measure delivery gaps and completed frames. A true zero-drop sensor claim also
needs a validated firmware frame counter or an independent sensor-rate oracle.
No RAW packing, Bayer order or nominal per-config FPS is guessed by this tool.
It tests RAW automatically if a verified RAW format is added to ENUM_FMT later.

Preserve the current firmware config dump, v4l2-ctl --all/--list-formats-ext,
the JSONL output, dmesg before/after, exact kernel and source hashes. Check both
P010 presets for correct image content separately; this runner deliberately
avoids a converter or disk writer in the capture loop.
